  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X+2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(H)={H}^{2}+H+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${H}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(H):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(H) & ={H}^{2}+H+2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22+5+2 & 4+1+0 \\\\ -12-3+0 & -2-1+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}29 & 5 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
